Let $\psi$ be a state on ${\mathcal O}_d$ and $({\mathcal H},\pi,\Omega)$ be the GNS space associated with $({\mathcal O}_d,\psi)$. We set $S_i=\pi(s_i)$ and normal state $\psi_{\Omega}$ on $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ defined by $$\psi_{\Omega}(X) = <\Omega, X \Omega>$$ Let $P$ be the projection on the closed subspace ${\mathcal K}$ generated by the vectors $\{S^*_I\Omega: |I| < \infty \}$ and  egin{equation} v_k=PS_kP \end{equation} for $1 \le k \le d$. Then following holds: \vskip 1em 
oindent (a) $\{v^*_I\Omega: |I| < \infty \}$ is total in ${\mathcal K}$. 
oindent (b) $\sum_{1 \le k \le d} v_kv_k^*=I$; 
oindent (c) $S_k^*P=PS_k^*P$ for all $1 \le k \le d$; 
oindent (d) For any $I=(i_1,i_2,...,i_k),J=(j_1,j_2,...,j_l)$ with $|I|,|J| < \infty$ we have  egin{equation} \psi(s_Is^*_J)= <\Omega,v_Iv^*_J\Omega> \end{equation} and the vectors $\{ S_If: f \in {\mathcal K},\;|I| < \infty \}$ are total in the GNS Hilbert space associated with $({\mathcal O}_d,\psi)$. Further such a family $({\mathcal K},\;v_k,\;1 \le k \le d,\;\omega)$ satisfying (a) to (d) is determined uniquely up to isomorphism. \vskip 1em Conversely given a Popescu system $({\mathcal K},v_k,\;1 \le k \le d,\Omega)$ satisfying (a) and (b) there exists a unique state $\psi$ on ${\mathcal O}_d$ so that (c) and (d) are satisfied. Furthermore the following statements are valid: \vskip 1em 
oindent (e) If the normal state $\phi(x)=<\Omega,x \Omega>$ on the von-Neumann algebra ${\mathcal M}=\{ v_i, v^*_i \}''$ is invariant for the Markov map $	au(x)=\sum_{1 \le k \le d} v_ixv^*_i,\;x \in {\mathcal M}$ then $\psi$ is $\lambda$ invariant and $\phi$ is faithful on ${\mathcal M}$. \vskip 1em 
oindent (f) If $P \in \pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ then following are equivalent: 
oindent (i) $\psi$ is an ergodic state for $({\mathcal O}_d,\lambda)$; 
oindent (ii) $({\mathcal M},	au,\phi)$ is ergodic; 
oindent (ii) ${\mathcal M}$ is a factor.

(a) is a restatement of Proposition 3.2 (a). ${\mathcal E}$ ( $	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$ ) being the support projection of the state $\psi$ in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ ( $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ ) and $\psi = \psi \Lambda$ we have $\Lambda({\mathcal E}) \ge {\mathcal E}$ and further we have ${\mathcal E}=[\pi({\mathcal O}_d)'\Omega] \ge [\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''\Omega]$ and hence increasing projections in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$, $\Lambda^n({\mathcal E}) \uparrow I$ as $n ightarrow \infty$ because $\Omega$ is cyclic for $\pi({\mathcal O}_d \otimes 	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ in ${\mathcal H} \otimes_{{\mathcal K}} 	ilde{{\mathcal H}}$ and limiting projection being in the commutant of $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ as well i.e. since $\Lambda(Y)=Y$ implies $Y \in \pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ by Cuntz relations. \vskip 1em We set von-Neumann algebras ${\mathcal N}_1=\pi({\mathcal O}_d)'{\mathcal E}$ and ${\mathcal N}_2=\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''{\mathcal E}$. By our construction we have $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)'' \subseteq \pi({\mathcal O}_d)' $ and so ${\mathcal N}_2 \subseteq {\mathcal N}_1$. Since $\Lambda^n({\mathcal E}) \uparrow I$ as $n ightarrow \infty$ in strong operator topology, two operators in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)'$ are same if their actions are same on ${\mathcal E}$. So (b) is true. \vskip 1em For (c) we note that $Q={\mathcal E}	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \in {\mathcal N}_2 \subseteq {\mathcal N}_1$ and claim that $Q$ is the support projection of the state $\psi$ in ${\mathcal N}_2$. To that end let $x{\mathcal E} \ge 0$ for some $x \in \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ so that $\psi(QxQ)=0$. As $\Lambda^k(x{\mathcal E}) \ge 0$ for all $k \ge 1$ and $\Lambda^k({\mathcal E}) ightarrow I$ we conclude that $x \ge 0$. As ${\mathcal E} \Omega=\Omega$ and thus $\psi(	ilde{{\mathcal E}}x	ilde{{\mathcal E}})=\psi(QxQ)=0$, we conclude $	ilde{{\mathcal E}}x	ilde{{\mathcal E}}=0$, $	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$ being the support projection for $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$. Hence $QxQ=0$. As $\psi(Q)=1$, we complete the proof of the claim that $Q$ is the support of $\psi$ in ${\mathcal N}_2$. Similarly $Q$ is also the support projection of the state $\psi$ in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$. This completes the proof of (c). \vskip 1em Thus if ${\mathcal E} {\mathcal F}=	ilde{{\mathcal E}}	ilde{{\mathcal F}}$, we get $\Lambda^n({\mathcal E}){\mathcal F}=	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \Lambda^n(	ilde{{\mathcal F}})$ and ${\mathcal E} 	ilde{\Lambda}({\mathcal F}) = 	ilde{\Lambda}(	ilde{{\mathcal E}})	ilde{{\mathcal F}}$ and thus taking limit we get ${\mathcal F}=	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$ and ${\mathcal E}=	ilde{{\mathcal F}}$. It is obvious now that $P={\mathcal E} {\mathcal F} = {\mathcal E} 	ilde{{\mathcal E}}=Q$. This completes the proof of (d). \vskip 1em As ${\mathcal E} \in \pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \in \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ we check that von-Neumann algebras ${\mathcal M}^1 = Q \pi({\mathcal O}_d)'' Q$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1 = Q \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d) Q$ acting on $Q$ satisfy $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1 \subseteq {\mathcal M}^{1'}$. Now we explore that $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d)''= {\mathcal B}({\mathcal H} \otimes_{{\mathcal K}} 	ilde{{\mathcal H}})$ and note that in such a case $Q\pi( 	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d )''Q$ is the set of all bounded operators on the Hilbert subspace $Q$. As ${\mathcal E} \in \pi({\mathcal O}_d)''$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \in \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ we check that together ${\mathcal M}^1 = Q \pi({\mathcal O}_d)'' Q$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1 = Q \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d) Q$ generate all bounded operators on $Q$. Thus both ${\mathcal M}^1$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1$ are factors. The canonical states $\psi$ on ${\mathcal M}^1$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1$ are faithful and normal. We set $l_k=QS_kQ$ and $	ilde{l}_k=Q	ilde{S}_kQ,\; 1 \le k \le d$ and recall that $v_k=PS_kP$ and $	ilde{v}_k=P	ilde{S}_kP,\; 1 \le k \le d$. We note that $Pl_kP=v_k$ and $P	ilde{l}_kP = 	ilde{v}_k$ where we recall that, by our construction, $P$ is the support projection of the state $\psi$ in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''_{|}[\pi({\mathcal O}_d)\Omega]$. $Q$ being the support projection of $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$, by Theorem 2.4 applied to Cuntz elements $\{S_i	ilde{E}: 1 \le i \le d \}$, $	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \pi({\mathcal O}_d)'	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$ is order isomorphic to ${\mathcal M}^{1'}$ via the map $X ightarrow QXQ$. As the projection ${\mathcal F}=[\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''\Omega] \in \pi({\mathcal O}_d)'$, we check that the element $Q{\mathcal F}	ilde{{\mathcal E}}Q \in {\mathcal M}^{1'}$. However $Q{\mathcal F} 	ilde{{\mathcal E}}Q={\mathcal E}	ilde{{\mathcal E}}{\mathcal F}	ilde{{\mathcal E}}{\mathcal E}=QPQ=P$ and thus $P \in {\mathcal M}^{1'}$. We also check that ${\mathcal M}^1\Omega={\mathcal M}^1P\Omega=P{\mathcal M}^1\Omega={\mathcal M} \Omega$ and thus $P=[{\mathcal M}^1\Omega]$. We set $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ for the von-Neumann algebra generated by $\{	ilde{v}_k:\; 1 \le k \le d \}$. \vskip 1em So far our analysis did not use the hypothesis on statement (e) i.e. $P=Q$. For $P=Q$, we have ${\mathcal M}^1={\mathcal M}$ and $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}^1=	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$. By order isomorphic property we get (i) is equivalent to $	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \pi({\mathcal O}_d)' 	ilde{{\mathcal E}} = 	ilde{{\mathcal E}} \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$ and taking commutant again we get $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''	ilde{{\mathcal E}} = \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)'	ilde{{\mathcal E}}$. Now we invoke the first part of the argument changing the role or using the endomorphism $	ilde{\Lambda}$ we conclude that $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''=\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)'$. This completes the proof of (e) provided we find a independent proof for (i) which is not so evident and this crucial point was not noticed in the proof given for Lemma 7.8 in [BJKW]. \vskip 1em Now we will analyze the representation $\pi$ of $	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d$ which is pure to prove (i). To that end we note since $P=Q$ by our assumption, $\Omega$ is a common cyclic and separating vector for $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ and ${\mathcal M}'$. Thus we can get an endomorphism $\alpha: {\mathcal M}' ightarrow 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ defined by $$\alpha(y)=	ilde{{\mathcal J}}{\mathcal J} y {\mathcal J} 	ilde{{\mathcal J}}$$ where $	ilde{{\mathcal J}}$ is the Tomita's conjugate operator associated with the cyclic and separating vector $\Omega$ for $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ ( i.e. for $y \in {\mathcal M}'$, we have ${\mathcal J} y {\mathcal J} \in {\mathcal M} \subseteq 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}'$ since $	ilde{{\mathcal M}} \subseteq {\mathcal M}'$. Since $	ilde{{\mathcal J}}	ilde{{\mathcal M}}'	ilde{{\mathcal J}}=	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$, we have $\alpha(y) \in 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ ). We note that the general theory does not guarantee [AcC] that the endomorphism be Takesaki's canonical conditional expectation associated with $\phi$. If so then the modular automorphism group $(\sigma_t)$ of ${\mathcal M}'$ also preserves $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$. Thus $\sigma_z(x) \in 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ for $-1 \le Im(z) \le 1$ if $x$ is an analytic element in $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$. Thus we would have got ${\mathcal J} v_k(\delta) {\mathcal J} = \sigma_{i \over 2}(	ilde{v}_k(\delta)) \in 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ where $x(\delta)$ is average of $\sigma_t(x)$ with respect to Gaussian measure with variance $\delta > 0$. That $	ilde{v}_k(\delta)$ is an analytic element follows from the general Tomita-Takesaki theory [BR1]. Since $v_k(\delta) ightarrow v_k$ in strong operator topology as $\delta ightarrow 0$ and ${\mathcal M} = \{v_k: 1 \le k \le d \}''$ together with ${\mathcal J} {\mathcal M} {\mathcal J} = {\mathcal M}'$ we arrived at $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}={\mathcal M}'$. In the following we avoid this tempting route and aim to explore the general representation theory of $C^*$-algebras [BR1,chapter 2]. \vskip 1em We claim that ${\mathcal M}'=	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$. Suppose not i.e. $	ilde{{\mathcal M}} \subset {\mathcal M}'$. Then $\alpha(	ilde{{\mathcal M}})$ is a proper von-Neumann subalgebras of $\alpha({\mathcal M}') \subseteq 	ilde{{\mathcal M}} $ being an into map and hence $\alpha(	ilde{{\mathcal M}})$ is a proper von-Neumann sub-algebra of $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$. Now consider the Popescu elements $({\mathcal K},\alpha(	ilde{v}_i),\Omega)$ and its dilation as in Theorem 2.1. Then by the commutant lifting theorem applied to pairs $(	ilde{v}_i),\alpha(	ilde{v}_i)$ we find a unitary operator $U$ on $	ilde{{\mathcal H}}$ so that $U\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''U^*$ is strictly contained in $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ ( Without loss of generality we can take the dilated Hilbert space for $({\mathcal K},\alpha(	ilde{v}_i),\Omega)$ to be same as $	ilde{{\mathcal H}}$ as there exists an isomorphism preserving ${\mathcal K}$, see the remark that follows after Theorem 2.1 ). We extend $U$ to a unitary operator on $	ilde{{\mathcal H}} \otimes_{{\mathcal K}} {\mathcal H}$ and denote $\pi_u(x)=U \pi(x) U^*$ for $x \in 	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d$ which is unitary equivalent to the pure representation $\pi$ and $\pi_u(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$ is strictly contained in $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$. Now $\pi_u$ is also an amalgamated representation over the subspace ${\mathcal K}$ with $P_u=Q_u$. Thus we can repeat now same procedure with $\pi_u$ and so on. Note that the process won't terminate in finite time. Our aim is to find a contradiction from this using formal set theory. \vskip 1em To that end we use temporary notation $\pi_0$ for $\pi$ defined in last paragraph and $\pi$ will be used for a generic representation. Let ${\mathcal P}$ be the collection of representation $(\pi,H_{\pi},\Omega)$ quasi-equivalent to $\pi_0:	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d ightarrow {\mathcal B}(	ilde{{\mathcal H}} \otimes_{{\mathcal K}} {\mathcal H} )$ with a shift invariant vector state $\omega(x)=<\Omega,\pi(x)\Omega>$ i.e. $\omega(\pi(	heta(x))=\omega(\pi(x))$. So there exists cardinal numbers $n_{\pi},n_0(\pi)$ so that $n_{\pi}H_{\pi}$ is unitary equivalent to $n_0(\pi)\pi_0$. Thus given an element $(\pi,H_{\pi},\Omega)$ we can associate two cardinal numbers $n_{\pi}$ and $n_0(\pi)$ and without loss of generality we assume that $H_{\pi} \subseteq n_0(\pi)H_0$ and $n_{\pi}H_{\pi}=n_0(\pi)H_0.$ $\pi_0$ being a pure representation, any element $\pi \in {\mathcal P}$ is a type-I factor representation of $	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d $. The interesting point here that $\displaystyle{\oplus_{\pi \in {\mathcal P} }}\pi$ is also an element in ${\mathcal P}$ with associated cardinal numbers $\sum_{\pi} n_{\pi}$ and $\sum_{\pi} n_0(\pi)$. We say $(\pi_1,H_{\pi_1},\Omega^1) \prec (\pi_2,H_{\pi_2},\Omega^2)$ if there exists an isometry $U:n_{\pi_1} H_{\pi_1} ightarrow n_{\pi_2} H_{\pi_2}$ so that \vskip 1em 
oindent (C1) For each $1 \le \alpha \le n_{\pi_1} $ we have $U \Omega^1_{\alpha} = \Omega^2_{\alpha'}$ for some $1 \le \alpha' \le n_{\pi_2}$; 
oindent (C2) $n_{\pi_2}\pi_2(x)E'_2 = U n_{\pi_1}\pi_1(x) U^* $ where ${\mathcal E}'_2 \in n_{\pi_2}\pi_2(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)'$; 
oindent (C3) $U\oplus_{1 \le \alpha \le n_{\pi_1} }\pi^{\alpha}_1(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''U^* \subset \oplus_{1 \le \alpha \le n_{\pi_2}}\pi^{\alpha}_1(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''E'_2$. \vskip 1em That the partial order is non-reflexive follows as $(\pi,H_{\pi},\Omega) \prec (\pi,H_{\pi},\Omega)$ contradicts (C3) as $I=E_2'$. By our starting assumption that ${\mathcal M}' 
eq 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ we check that $\pi_0 \prec \pi_u$. Thus going via the isomorphism we also check that for a given element $\pi \in {\mathcal P}$ there exists an element $\pi' \in {\mathcal P}$ so that $\pi \prec \pi'$. Thus ${\mathcal P}_0$ is a non empty set and has at least one infinite chain. Partial order property follows easily. If $\pi_1 \prec \pi_2$ and $\pi_2 \prec \pi_3$ then $\pi_1 \prec \pi_3$. If $U_{12}$ and $U_{23}$ are isometric operators that satisfies (C1)-(C3) respectively, then $U_{13}=U_{23}U_{12}$ will do the job for $\pi_1$ and $\pi_3$. \vskip 1em However by Hausdorff maximality theorem there exists a non-empty maximal totally ordered subset ${\mathcal P}_0$ of ${\mathcal P}$. We claim that $\pi_{max}=\oplus_{\pi \in {\mathcal P}_0} \pi$ on $H_{\pi_{max}}=\oplus_{\pi \in {\mathcal P}_0} H_{\pi} $ is an upper bound in ${\mathcal P}_0$. That $\pi_{max} \in {\mathcal P}$ is obvious. Further given an element $(H_1,\pi_1,\Omega_1) \in {\mathcal P}_0$ there exists an element $(H_2,\pi_2,\Omega_2) \in {\mathcal P}_0$ so that $\pi_1 \prec \pi_2$ by our starting remark as $\pi_0 \prec \pi_u$. By extending isometry $U_{12}$ to an isometry from $H_1 ightarrow n_{\pi_{max}} H_{\pi_{max}}$ trivially we get the required isometry that satisfies (C1),(C2) and (C3) where cardinal numbers $n_{\pi_{max}} = \sum_{\pi \in {\mathcal P}_0} n_{\pi} \in \aleph_0$. Thus by maximal property of ${\mathcal P}_0$ we have $\pi_{max} \in {\mathcal P}_0$. This brings a contradiction as by our construction $(\pi_{max},H_{\pi_{max}},\Omega) \prec (\pi_{max},H_{\pi_{max}},\Omega)$ as $\pi_{max} \in {\mathcal P}_0$ but partial order is strict. This contradicts our starting hypothesis that $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ is a proper subset of ${\mathcal M}'$. This completes the proof for (i) of (e) ${\mathcal M}'=	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ when $P=Q$. \vskip 1em In the proof of ${\mathcal M}'=	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ in (e), we have used equality $P=Q$ just to ensure that $\Omega$ is also a cyclic for $	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ and $P=Q$ is used to prove $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)' = \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$. So (f) follows by the proof of (e). \vskip 1em A proof for (g) is given in [Mo3] with ${\mathcal M}_0$. Here we will also give an alternative proof relating the criteria obtained in Proposition 3.4. To that end we claim that $$ igcap_{n \ge 1}	ilde{\Lambda}^n(\pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)')=\pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d)'  igcap \pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)'.$$ That $	ilde{\Lambda}^n(\pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)') \subseteq \{ 	ilde{S}_I	ilde{S}^*_J: |I|=|J| < \infty \}'$ follows by Cuntz relation and thus $ igcap_{n \ge 1}	ilde{\Lambda}^n(\pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)') \subseteq \pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d)' igcap \pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)'$. For the reverse inclusion let $X \in \pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d)' igcap \pi(\mbox{UHF}_d)'$. For $n \ge 1$, we choose $|I|=n$ and set $Y_n=	ilde{S}^*_IX	ilde{S}_I$. We check that it is independent of the index that we have chosen as $Y_n=	ilde{S}^*_IX	ilde{S}_I	ilde{S}^*_J	ilde{S}_J=	ilde{S}^*_I	ilde{S}_I	ilde{S}^*_JX 	ilde{S}_J=	ilde{S}^*_JX	ilde{S}_J$ where in second equality we have used $X \in \pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d)'$ and also $\Lambda^n(Y_n)=\sum_{|J|=n } 	ilde{S}_J	ilde{S}^*_IX	ilde{S}_I	ilde{S}^*_J=X$. This proves the equality in the claim. Going along the same line we also get $$ igcap_{n \ge 1}	ilde{\Lambda}^n(\pi({\mathcal O}_d)') = \pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d)' igcap \pi({\mathcal O}_d)'= \pi(	ilde{\mbox{UHF}}_d \otimes {\mathcal O}_d)'.$$ \vskip 1em By Proposition 3.4 $\omega$ is pure if and only if the set above is trivial. Thus once more by Proposition 1.1 in [Ar2] and Theorem 2.4 in [Mo2], purity is equivalent to asymptotic relation $||\psi 	ilde{	au}^n -\phi|| ightarrow 0$ as $n ightarrow \infty$ for any normal state $\psi$ on ${\mathcal M}'$ ( Here we recall by Proposition 2.4 $P\pi({\mathcal O}_d)'P={\mathcal M}'$ as $P$ is also the support projection in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''{\mathcal F}$ and support projection of $\phi_{\Omega}$ in $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)'$ is ${\mathcal F}=[\pi({\mathcal O}_d)''\Omega]$, where commutant is taken in ${\mathcal B}({\mathcal K})$ ). By a duality argument Theorem 2.4 in [Mo3] we conclude that $\omega$ is pure if and only if there exists a sequence of elements $x_n \in {\mathcal M}$ so that for each $m \ge 0$, $x_{m+n}	au_n(x) ightarrow \phi(x)1$ as $n ightarrow \infty$ for all $x \in {\mathcal M} \subseteq {\mathcal B}({\mathcal K})$. This completes the proof of (d) with ${\mathcal M}$. For the proof with ${\mathcal M}_0$ we need to show if part as only if part follows ${\mathcal M}_0$ being a subset of ${\mathcal M}$ and $	au$ takes elements of ${\mathcal M}_0$ to itself. For if part we refer to Theorem 3.2 in [Mo3].

Let $\omega$ be as in Theorem 3.5. Then the following holds: \vskip 1em 
oindent (a) $P$ is also the support projection of $\psi$ in $\pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d))''_{|}	ilde{{\mathcal H}}$ if and only if $\omega$ is pure. \vskip 1em 
oindent (b) If $\omega$ is pure then the following holds: 
oindent (i) ${\mathcal M}'= 	ilde{{\mathcal M}}$ where $	ilde{{\mathcal M}} = \{ P	ilde{S}_iP: 1 \le i \le d \}''$; 
oindent (ii) $\pi({\mathcal O}_d)' = \pi(	ilde{{\mathcal O}}_d)''$. 
oindent (iii) $\pi_{\omega}({\mathcal B}_R)' = \pi_{\omega}({\mathcal B}_L)''$;

